```java
public interface ConfigProvider {
    Optional<String> get(String key);
    default int getInt(String key, int fallback) {
        return get(key).map(Integer::parseInt).orElse(fallback);
    }
}

public class EnvConfigProvider implements ConfigProvider {
    public Optional<String> get(String key) { return Optional.ofNullable(System.getenv(key)); }
}

public class FileConfigProvider implements ConfigProvider {
    private final Properties props;
    public Optional<String> get(String key) { return Optional.ofNullable(props.getProperty(key)); }
}
```

`get` returns an `Optional`, not a raw nullable string or a thrown exception — pushing the missing-key decision (default, error, or optional) to the caller rather than baking one choice into the provider.

```java
public class LiveConfigProvider implements ConfigProvider {
    private volatile Map<String, String> current;

    public LiveConfigProvider(ConfigSource source) {
        this.current = source.load();
        source.onChange(next -> this.current = next);
    }

    public Optional<String> get(String key) {
        return Optional.ofNullable(current.get(key));
    }
}
```

The `volatile` field is the whole trick: a background watcher swaps in a new immutable map atomically, and every `get` call simply reads whatever's current — callers never see a half-updated config, and never know a reload even happened.
